.TH CSTR_SUBSTRING 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cstr_substring \- create a new string from a sub-range, and call ccol_fatal_err() on failure
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>

cstr result = cstr_substring(s, start, len);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a GNU C statement expression. It calls cstring_substring(). It
then calls ccol_fatal_err() if the result is NULL, which means that an
allocation failed. The macro gives the new cstring as a value. You can
therefore use the macro directly inside an expression.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
s      Source cstr
start  Zero-based start index
len    Number of characters to include
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new cstr that holds the sub-range that you asked for
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This macro uses a GNU C statement expression ({...}), so it needs GCC or
Clang
The macro clamps the range at the end of the string, and it does not reject
the range
The caller must destroy the cstring that this macro returns
The macro evaluates each of s, start and len exactly once. The failure
message therefore reports the start and the length that the call used
Call cstring_substring() directly when you want to handle an error
yourself
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cstr_construct(src, "Hello, world!");
cstr sub = cstr_substring(src, 7, 5);   /* "world" */
printf("%s\en", cstr_c_str(sub));
cstr_destroy(sub);
cstr_destroy(src);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cstring_substring (3),
.BR cstr_copy (3),
.BR cstr_split (3)
